Since the inclined surface is smooth, mechanical energy is conserved. The object starts from rest at the top, with zero potential energy (as defined) and zero kinetic energy, so the total mechanical energy is 0 J at the top. At the midpoint, the object has descended a vertical height of \( h = \frac{2}{2} \sin 30^\circ = 0.5 \, \text{m} \) below the reference point, giving it a potential energy of \( PE = mgh = 1 \times 10 \times (-0.5) = -5 \, \text{J} \) (taking \( g = 10 \, \text{m/s}^2 \)). Conservation of mechanical energy requires \( KE + PE = 0 \), so \( KE = 5 \, \text{J} \). Thus, the mechanical energy remains 0 J, and the kinetic energy is 5 J.

